  "method": "Series: for every year CDC publishes (2015 on), the CDC VSRR 'Number of Drug Overdose Deaths' for jurisdiction New York City, 12 months ending December, reported (data_value, not predicted_value), so the definition is the same in every year. dohmh_series (context only, not scored): counts read from the chart in Epi Data Brief No. 150 (unintentional drug poisoning deaths, New York City, 2000 to 2024), checked against the brief's key finding for 2024. Peers: CDC county file (gb4e-yj24), year 2025, month 12, divided by Census Vintage 2025 POPESTIMATE2025, times 100,000.",
  "caveats": [
    "The 2025 value is CDC provisional and could change when final data are released. CDC's file notes for 2015-2024 that the numbers may differ from published reports using final data.",
    "CDC's series starts in 2015, so history is shorter than the Health Department's. CDC counts overdose deaths of all intents (ICD-10 X40-X44, X60-X64, X85, Y10-Y14) by the jurisdiction where the death occurred.",
    "For context, the file also carries the Health Department's own count (dohmh_series, 2000-2024): unintentional overdoses only, ages 15-84, deaths in the city; it labels 2023 and 2024 provisional. Where both exist CDC runs a little higher: 2015: CDC 959 vs Health Department 942 (+1.8%); 2016: CDC 1,424 vs Health Department 1,413 (+0.8%); 2017: CDC 1,493 vs Health Department 1,482 (+0.7%); 2018: CDC 1,482 vs Health Department 1,452 (+2.1%); 2019: CDC 1,548 vs Health Department 1,497 (+3.4%); 2020: CDC 2,131 vs Health Department 2,103 (+1.3%); 2021: CDC 2,775 vs Health Department 2,696 (+2.9%); 2022: CDC 3,142 vs Health Department 3,070 (+2.3%); 2023: CDC 3,111 vs Health Department 3,056 (+1.8%); 2024: CDC 2,254 vs Health Department 2,192 (+2.8%).",
    "County peer counts suppress values of 1-9; none of the peer counties are affected."
  ]
